Prepare your #cupcake tin{} by lining it with muffin liners and preheat your oven to 175℃ / 347℉ (no fan).

With an electric hand mixer, whip together the wet ingredients for ~{1-2%minutes}: @eggs{2}(room temperature), @vegetable oil{1/2%cup}(can sub melted butter), @granulated sugar{1/2%cup}, @brown sugar{1/2%cup}, @coffee{1/2%cup}(freshly brewed strong coffee, room temperature), @whole milk{1/2%cup}(room temperature), and @vanilla extract{1%teaspoon}.

Sift dry ingredients together: @all purpose flour{2%cups}, @baking powder{1/2%teaspoon}(heaping if you like fluffier muffins), @baking soda{1/2%teaspoon}, @cocoa powder{1/2%cup}(unsweetened, dutch processed), and @salt{1/2%teaspoon}(omit if using salted butter), then pour the wet ingredients into the dry ingredients and fold just until combined.

Mix in about 2/3 of the @chocolate chips{1%cup}(use semi-sweet chocolate chips or chunks).

Spoon the batter evenly into the cavities of your cupcake tin, filling each one about ¾ full. Then, spread @chocolate chips{1/4%cup}(use semi-sweet chocolate chips or chunks) on top evenly between the 12 cavities.

Bake the double chocolate muffins for ~{20-25%minutes} or until a toothpick inserted into the center comes out clean. Do not overbake them, as they can dry out.

Cool the baked muffins on a cooling rack and serve them fresh. Any leftovers can be stored in an airtight container at room temperature for 2-3 days.

> Store leftovers in an airtight container at room temperature for 2-3 days or in the fridge for a week.